reg — Linear regression by ordinary least squares                   regress · tier 2 · since 0.4.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    data.reg(formula, se: classical | robust | hc2 | hc3, cluster: Var, weights: Var,
             weight_type: frequency | analytic | sampling = sampling, level: Float = 0.95)

DESCRIPTION
    Fits the formula by ordinary least squares on the rows of the frame without `NA` in a model variable,
    which are dropped and counted, and prints the summary of section 10.2 of the Language Reference: the
    formula, the rows used, the kind of standard error, a table with each term's estimate, standard error, t
    value, p-value and confidence interval, and R² with its adjusted value. The formula's terms follow
    section 7.2: `a * b` expands, a category gets its indicator columns by its contrast with the first level
    as the base unless `base()` names another, `exper^2` and `log(wage)` are computed before the fit, and a
    term written twice is kept once with a note. A regressor collinear with the columns before it is dropped
    and named in a note; a design whose scaled condition number passes 1e8 is warned about (W1001). Standard
    errors are classical at this release; robust and clustered errors, weights and fixed effects follow in
    0.4.0 and 0.6.0. Like R's `lm` and Stata's `regress`.

ARGUMENTS
    data          Frame. The frame.
    formula       Formula. `y ~ terms`, written in the call or bound with `let`.
    se:           classical | robust | hc2 | hc3. Standard-error type; `robust` is HC1, as Stata's `robust`;
                  `bootstrap` from 1.1. With sampling weights the default is `robust`, and `se: classical`
                  is an error (Z0410). With `weight_type: frequency`, classical errors are valid and the
                  observation count is the sum of the weights. The kind of standard error: `classical`,
                  `robust` (HC1), `hc2` or `hc3`; `classical` unless sampling weights are given, when
                  `robust`.                                                                                   (shared)
    cluster:      Var. Cluster-robust standard errors (CR1); one-observation clusters are reported; two-way
                  and multiway clustering arrive after 1.0. The variable whose values group the observations
                  for clustered errors (CR1).                                                                 (shared)
    weights:      Var. Weight variable. The weight variable.                                                  (shared)
    weight_type:  frequency | analytic | sampling = sampling. Stata's fweight, aweight and pweight. In 1.0
                  the tests refuse `weights:` with `weight_type: sampling` (Z0411) and point to regression
                  with robust errors. What the weights are: `frequency` counts, `analytic` precision
                  weights, `sampling` probability weights.                                                    (shared)
    level:        Float = 0.95. Confidence level. The confidence level of the intervals.                      (shared)

VALUE
    A `RegFit`, which prints as its summary.

    Methods: none in this release

DETAILS
    Each regressor is scaled to unit norm before a blocked Householder QR of the design and the outcome
    together, so that the fit of a column at 1e12 is as accurate as one at 1, and no n × k matrix is held;
    the k × k R factor is then factorised again with column pivoting, a column whose remaining norm is below
    1e-7 of its own being dropped as collinear with those before it, which is R's rule. The estimates come
    from back substitution and are unscaled; residuals, fitted values and leverages come from a second pass
    over the rows. The classical variance is the residual sum of squares over n − k times (XᵀX)⁻¹, read from
    R; `robust` is HC1, the sandwich of the scores scaled by n/(n − k), as Stata's `robust` and R's
    `sandwich::vcovHC(type = "HC1")`; `hc2` and `hc3` scale each observation's score by 1/(1 − h) and 1/(1 −
    h)², with h its leverage; `cluster:` sums the scores within each cluster (CR1) and applies Stata's
    adjustment G/(G − 1) · (n − 1)/(n − k), with the t table on G − 1 degrees of freedom, as Stata's
    `regress` and reghdfe do and as `sandwich::vcovCL(type = "HC1")` gives; the other kinds use n − k.
    Weights follow section 6: frequency weights count each row as many times as its weight, so N is their
    sum; analytic weights are precision weights, scaled to sum to the rows; sampling weights are probability
    weights, with which `se:` defaults to `robust` and `classical` is refused. A row with `NA` in the weight
    or the cluster is dropped with the others; a zero weight leaves its row out, with a note; a negative
    weight is an error. After every fit the residuals are checked to be orthogonal to the regressors and the
    variance matrix to be symmetric with no negative variance, a failure being an internal error rather than
    a result.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Note    a term written twice kept once; a collinear column dropped; a category of one level; rows with a
            zero weight left out; clusters of one observation
    W1001   near-singular regressors, with the condition number
    W1002   fewer than 30 clusters
    W1003   extreme weights, with the effective sample size
    W1006   an observation whose removal moves an estimate by more than its standard error
    Z0101   a variable of the formula, `cluster:` or `weights:` is not in the frame
    Z0223   an infinite value in a model variable
    Z0224   a negative weight
    Z0304   `se: bootstrap` before release 1.1, or a date in the model before 0.6.0
    Z0401   text in the model, a category as the outcome, a level outside 0 to 1, a weight that is not a
            number, or a frequency weight with a fraction
    Z0410   `se: classical` with sampling weights, or `se: hc2` or `hc3` with `cluster:`
    Z0801   no row remains after the rows with NA are dropped
    Z0802   no regressor remains, no residual degrees of freedom, or one cluster

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Davidson, R. and MacKinnon, J. G. (2004). *Econometric Theory and Methods*. Oxford University
    Press, chapters 2 and 3. MacKinnon, J. G. and White, H. (1985). Some heteroskedasticity-consistent
    covariance matrix estimators with improved finite sample properties. *Journal of Econometrics*,
    29, 305–325. Cameron, A. C. and Miller, D. L. (2015). A practitioner's guide to cluster-robust
    inference. *Journal of Human Resources*, 50, 317–372.

SEE ALSO
    category, base, interact, summary

EXAMPLES
    > let wages = dataset(wages)
    > wages.reg(log(wage) ~ educ + exper + exper^2 + female)
    reg · log(wage) ~ educ + exper + exper^2 + female
    N = 18,179 (233 rows dropped for NA in model variables)
    standard errors: classical

      term          estimate   std.error   t value   p value   95% CI
      (intercept)     1.7873      0.0236     75.74    <0.001   [1.7410, 1.8335]
      educ            0.0758      0.0014     55.80    <0.001   [0.0731, 0.0785]
      exper           0.0421      0.0016     26.36    <0.001   [0.0389, 0.0452]
      exper^2        -0.0007      0.0001    -13.09    <0.001   [-0.0008, -0.0006]
      female         -0.1319      0.0064    -20.63    <0.001   [-0.1445, -0.1194]

    R² 0.210 · adjusted R² 0.210
